// @test: typecheck-fail
// @expected-error: Type mismatch: expected 'Int', found 'Maybe<Int>'
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: types, coercion, maybe, soundness, silent-wrong-value
// @timeout: 60000
// @description: Wrapping an already-`Maybe` expression in `Maybe.Some(...)`
// must not be accepted (T0722).
//
// `List.first()` returns `Maybe<Int>`, so `Maybe.Some(xs.first())` builds a
// `Maybe<Maybe<Int>>` where the signature promises `Maybe<Int>`. This was
// accepted, and codegen emitted no coercion, so the payload stored was the
// inner Maybe. Measured then: the program printed 49753132273 instead of 43,
// with NO panic, NO warning and exit 0 — the Maybe's raw representation read
// as an `Int` and added to. The wrong value was never stable (49753132273,
// 42272801681, 31272579313, 48926250337 across runs), because it is a
// pointer. A test that merely RAN this code, rather than checking its
// output, passed the whole time.
//
// This file was RED ON PURPOSE while the hole was open, and its own text
// required that if the fix turned out to be rejection, it be converted to a
// typecheck-fail test in the same commit. That is what happened.
//
// THE ACCEPTANCE WAS NOT IN THE COMPILER. It was declared by the standard
// library: `core/base/maybe.vr` carried `implement<T> IntCoercible for
// Maybe<T> {}` — "a Maybe may stand in for an Int" — and `unify.rs` merely
// honoured it. `List`, `Range` and `RangeInclusive` declared the same thing.
// All four cited the same premise: that indexing and slicing leave a
// CONTAINER where an element is meant. Measured false — an index expression
// yields the element, and legitimate `xs[0]`, `xs.len()`, `for i in 0..3`
// and `for j in 1..=3` all work unchanged without those declarations.
//
// Three sibling files pin the rest of the family:
//   * `indexable_collection_not_int.vr`  — a collection is not an `Int`.
//   * `L2-standard/base/coercion_marker_refusal.vr` — a type WITHOUT a
//     marker is refused (the direction that was already covered).
//   * this file — a `Maybe` is not an `Int`, at the site where the payload
//     of a constructor is checked.

mount core.prelude.*;

fn head_plus_one(xs: &List<Int>) -> Maybe<Int> {
    if xs.is_empty() {
        return Maybe.None;
    }
    // `xs.first()` is ALREADY `Maybe<Int>`. Wrapping it again promises
    // `Maybe<Int>` while building `Maybe<Maybe<Int>>`; the correct spelling
    // is `xs.first()` on its own.
    Maybe.Some(xs.first())
}

fn main() {
    let mut xs: List<Int> = List.new();
    xs.push(42);
    match head_plus_one(&xs) {
        Maybe.Some(v) => { print(v + 1); }
        Maybe.None => { print("empty"); }
    }
}
